an't change the B6 pung.\n\nSo stuck with a pung.\n\nThus, **All Chows** impossible.\n\nBest path: minimize risk, discard a terminal.\n\nBetween T1 and B9, B9 is safer due to prior discard.\n\nW6: is it safe? Player across has [W6,W7,W8], so they completed that chow. They are unlikely to need another W6. I have one, and one unshown. Risky but possible.\n\nBut W6 is a simple tile, could be useful.\n\nT1 is terminal, more likely to be kept by opponents for waits.\n\nSimilarly B9.\n\nBut B9 already discarded by me.\n\nMoreover, if I keep W6 and W7, I can wait for W5 or W8 to form a chow.\n\nFor example, draw W5 \u2192 then [W5,W6,W7] chow, pair W7, discard T1 or B9.\n\nSimilarly, W8 \u2192 [W6,W7,W8].\n\nW8 unshown: 2, so possible.\n\nW5: 3 unshown.\n\nGood prospects.\n\nSo better to keep W6 and W7.\n\nThus, must discard either T1 or B9.\n\nChoose the safer terminal.\n\nB9: I discarded it, no one claimed \u2192 likely safe.\n\nT1: never discarded by me visibly, but I did discard it? No \u2014 I used one in chow, and now hold one.\n\nDiscard record: I played F1, F4, B2, B3, J1, F1, B8, J2, W9, W5, W1, T9 \u2014 no T1 discard.\n\nSo T1 has not been discarded by anyone \u2014 only one in my meld.\n\nMore dangerous.\n\nTherefore, **B9 is safer to discard**.\n\nReason: B9 has already been discarded by me without being claimed, reducing its value to opponents, while T1 remains unseen and is a terminal, posing higher risk if retained. Additionally, keeping W6 and W7 gives potential for a Character-suit chow, and maintaining the W7 pair supports a future win condition.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
